{
  "abstract": "Tao's almost-all Collatz theorem uses a decay estimate $|\\chi_n(\\xi)|\\ll_A n^{-A}$ for the characteristic function $\\chi_n(\\xi)=\\mathbb{E}\\,e(\\xi\\,\\mathrm{Syrac}(\\mathbb{Z}/3^n)/3^n)$ of the Syracuse random variable. We study the true size of $M_n=\\max_{3\\nmid\\xi}|\\chi_n(\\xi)|$. (1) A renewal identity expresses $\\chi_n(2^ju)$ through a cold-walk term and the lower-level values $\\chi_\\ell(u)$, weighted by the first time the walk $s_m$ exceeds $j$. (2) A conditional transfer theorem: if $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|\\le B\\rho^\\ell$ with $\\rho<\\lambda-1\\approx0.585$ ($\\lambda=\\log_23$), then $|\\chi_n(2^j)|\\le Ce^{0.3036\\,j}(\\lambda-1)^n$, which equals $Ce^{-In}$ at $j=\\lambda n$, with $I=\\lambda(\\ln2-H(1/\\lambda))\\approx0.05498$. (3) An $O(n^2)$-operation algorithm evaluates a single coefficient, with absolute truncation control. Float values of $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|$ for $\\ell\\le800$ decay like $e^{-0.565\\ell}$. Ball arithmetic certifies the upper envelope $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|\\le5.6961\\cdot0.58^\\ell$ for $\\ell\\le1000$, which proves the bound of (2) along $\\xi=2^j$ for all $n\\le1000$ with $C=39$ (computer-assisted). (4) We compute $M_n$ for $n\\le18$, and the maximum $M_n^*$ along $\\xi=2^j$ near $j=\\lambda n$ up to $n=2000$. Fitted rates are $0.0552$–$0.0553$; the polynomial correction is undetermined. Multiplier scans at $n=40,80,150$ support extremality of this family, and certified lower bounds at six levels lie within a factor $1.6\\times10^4$ of the upper bound. We conjecture $\\frac1n\\log M_n\\to-I$. This rate is ten times slower than the rate $\\tfrac12\\ln3$ of a random map that keeps the geometric input weights. Limitations: no bound for all $n$; extremality beyond $n=18$ and relative accuracy of large-$n$ values are supported only numerically; and Fourier decay is not what limits the density exponent in Tao's argument, so these results do not strengthen his theorem.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v2 implements the five v1 review corrections plus attribution. (1) Accuracy vs envelope: large-l float values are described as cutoff-stable only; the certificate is stated as an upper envelope (absolute truncation tail can exceed the value), not as relative certification; O(n^2) counts operations at the chosen cutoff. (2) Directed rounding: certify_chi1.py/certify_M.py now export bounds with outward decimal rounding (dfmt: nextafter then ROUND_CEILING/FLOOR); certify_chi1 gains column 8 (rigorous lower bound incl. tail). All certificates rerun (results/certify_chi1*.txt, certify_M.txt, cert_constant.txt); C<=38.9467 and B(0.58)=5.6960558 at l=131 unchanged to 1e-9; v1 outputs kept in results/v1_backup/. (3) Random-map comparison: unweighted model gives rms 2^{-n}; the 3^{-n/2} (rate 1/2 ln3) scale is attributed to the weighted model and no longer credited to Tao. (4) Section 5 cold-region inequalities corrected to lambda(m-1)+d<=s_m<=lambda n+d; bridge/ballot explanation marked heuristic; 'must come from other frequencies' replaced by the narrower pairing-majorant observation. (5) Removed the false 'lifts a level-(n-1) extremizer' claim; separated strong conjecture C1 from the weaker (1/n)log M_n -> -I; harmonised 800 (float scan) vs 1000 (certified) and 'six levels' of certified lower bounds; abstract shortened. Attribution: Tao Lemma 1.12, Remarks 1.15/1.18 and Section 6 credited for the recursion, mixing suggestion and stopping-time decomposition. Related work: Siegel-O'Dwyer [7] is cited for their claim, with a note that the proof of their passage-location comparison appears incomplete as written in v1 (internal contradiction for S = whole ball). Supplement rebuilt with the v2 outputs.",
